9. PHOTO UPLOAD — ARCHITECTURE
============================================================

Photos are NEVER stored as blobs in the SQLite database.
The database stores ONLY the URL or server path of the image.

Upload flow:
  1. User picks photo via MediaPicker (MAUI built-in)
  2. Show thumbnail preview in the form immediately (local file)
  3. On form submit: ArgazkiIgotzeZerbitzua uploads the file to Cloudinary
  4. Cloudinary returns a public HTTPS URL (string)
  5. Store that URL in BidaiaTxostena.ArgazkiUrl (string? column)
  6. If upload fails: show error Toast, allow retry, do not save null URL
     silently — ask user whether to submit without photo or retry

Server upload implementation (Cloudinary):
  - ArgazkiIgotzeZerbitzua uploads to Cloudinary. Load CLOUDINARY_CLOUD_NAME,
    CLOUDINARY_UPLOAD_PRESET (unsigned preset for mobile), and any signing keys
    from environment / secure config only — never hardcode in .cs or rules.
  - Typical client flow: POST multipart to
    https://api.cloudinary.com/v1_1/{cloud_name}/image/upload with file + upload_preset.
  - Store the HTTPS asset URL returned by Cloudinary (e.g. secure_url) in ArgazkiUrl.
  - Use HttpClient with MultipartFormDataContent (or Cloudinary .NET SDK if you add it).
  - Inject IHttpClientFactory, never create HttpClient with new()
  - Set timeout: 30 seconds for upload
  - Accepted formats: JPEG, PNG only — validate before upload
  - Max file size: 10 MB — validate before upload, show clear error if exceeded
  - Use a unique public_id when calling the API if you need predictable naming; otherwise rely on Cloudinary response URLs only
  - Register IHttpClientFactory in MauiProgram.cs

DB column for photo:
  BidaiaTxostena.ArgazkiUrl — string? (nullable)
  A null value means "no photo attached" — handle gracefully in UI

